WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.599 --> 00:00:05.950
Movies like Batman Begins and Batman versus
Superman: Dawn of Justice are full of scenes

00:00:05.950 --> 00:00:09.320
lifted directly from the pages of DC Comics.

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:15.260
But as the DC Extended Universe ramps up towards
the next Batman solo film, which is expected

00:00:15.260 --> 00:00:21.930
to hit theaters in 2019, director Matt Reeves
has over 75 years worth of Batman stories

00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:23.039
to pick from.

00:00:23.039 --> 00:00:25.910
And some of them definitely don't make the
grade.

00:00:25.910 --> 00:00:30.810
Here's a look at some Batman moments that
will never be on the big screen.

00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:32.619
Messiah of the Crimson Sun

00:00:32.619 --> 00:00:37.409
If you ever wished the James Bond flick Moonraker
had starred Batman instead, then boy, do we

00:00:37.409 --> 00:00:39.760
have a story for you.

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:45.289
Appearing in Batman Annual #8, "Messiah of
the Crimson Son" involves Ra's al Ghul making

00:00:45.289 --> 00:00:47.819
a giant space laser.

00:00:47.819 --> 00:00:53.249
But after commandeering a Space Shuttle and
engaging in some zero-gravity karate, Batman

00:00:53.249 --> 00:00:58.769
defeated the leader of the League of Assassins
by tractor beaming him into the laser blast.

00:00:58.769 --> 00:01:04.120
Considering the days of high camp seem to
in Batman's distant past, it's pretty unlikely

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:06.390
we'll ever see this one on screen.

00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:07.900
The Hunchback of the Batcave

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:12.500
"Where does he get those wonderful toys?"

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:17.430
In the movies, the Dark Knight's seemingly
endless arsenal of crimefighting equipment

00:01:17.430 --> 00:01:23.600
was either provided by Lucius Fox or made
down in the Batcave by Alfred and Batman himself.

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:27.600
It's a pretty good answer... as long as you
don't think about it too hard.

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:33.549
Why nobody down in WayneTech R&amp;D realizes
they spend an unusual amount of time sewing

00:01:33.549 --> 00:01:38.470
up wearable hang-glider capes and bat-shaped
boomerangs is anyone's guess.

00:01:38.470 --> 00:01:39.470
But honestly?

00:01:39.470 --> 00:01:43.090
It actually makes way more sense than what
we got in the comics.

00:01:43.090 --> 00:01:48.929
See, in the comics, all this stuff came courtesy
of a hunchbacked mute mechanical genius who

00:01:48.929 --> 00:01:52.360
lived in the Batcave and spent his life building
crap for Batman.

00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:57.479
Yep, it's true: this modern day, high tech
Quasimodo was named Harold Allnut, and while

00:01:57.479 --> 00:02:02.899
he was essential to keeping the Batcave operation
going, he almost never got any screentime

00:02:02.899 --> 00:02:06.650
at all, until he was unceremoniously murdered.

00:02:06.650 --> 00:02:08.930
The less said about Allnut, the better.

00:02:08.930 --> 00:02:10.690
Trial of the Bat-Witch

00:02:10.690 --> 00:02:16.530
Want to know the real reason Batman and Superman
seem to always be at each other's throats?

00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:23.490
Well, one unlikely explanation was provided
in the pages of 1969's World's Finest #186,

00:02:23.490 --> 00:02:29.520
where the Man of Steel and the Dark Knight
head back in time to find out why real-life

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:34.320
Revolutionary War hero "Mad" Anthony Wayne
looks like Batman.

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:39.350
The answer, of course, is that they're related,
but along the way, Superman claims to be a

00:02:39.350 --> 00:02:44.830
traveling actor by announcing that the S on
his chest stands for "Shakespeare" and then

00:02:44.830 --> 00:02:51.230
sets about convincing the good people of 1776
that Batman is a witch.

00:02:51.230 --> 00:02:56.120
This might seem out of character for Superman,
but there's actually a really good reason

00:02:56.120 --> 00:02:59.340
for it: He was possessed by a genie.

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:00.340
Obviously.

00:03:00.340 --> 00:03:01.340
Nocturna

00:03:01.340 --> 00:03:06.230
Hey, remember the time Batman had a custody
battle with a vampire over who got to raise

00:03:06.230 --> 00:03:07.230
Robin?

00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:08.230
No?

00:03:08.230 --> 00:03:11.010
Well, let us introduce you to Nocturna.

00:03:11.010 --> 00:03:15.990
After adopting Jason Todd, who was Robin at
the time, this bloodthirsty arch-criminal

00:03:15.990 --> 00:03:21.280
then tried to convince Batman that Robin could
only be raised properly if they got married.

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:27.370
A supernatural rom-com version of Kramer Versus
Kramer with Robin stuck in the middle?

00:03:27.370 --> 00:03:28.830
Someone call Katherine Heigl!

00:03:28.830 --> 00:03:30.450
A Christmas Story

00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:35.360
If you think the story of Christmas begins
with a manger, well, you've clearly never

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:41.030
read Batman: The Brave and the Bold #12, which
features the insane tale of how Christmas

00:03:41.030 --> 00:03:42.410
truly came to be.

00:03:42.410 --> 00:03:46.290
The story starts with Earth being destroyed
and just gets weirder from there.

00:03:46.290 --> 00:03:51.730
By the end of it, Batman and his partner Adam
Strange end up literally rebuilding the universe

00:03:51.730 --> 00:03:56.570
from within a reality-altering antimatter
stream, coincidentally made up of glowing

00:03:56.570 --> 00:04:00.110
red and green balls of light that twists their
bodies.

00:04:00.110 --> 00:04:05.990
Strange ends up being stretched out and aged,
with his rotund, bearded appearance imprinting

00:04:05.990 --> 00:04:11.280
on the subconscious mind of the rebuilt universe,
creating the idea of Santa Claus.

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:12.500
And Batman?

00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:15.190
Well, Batman is actually his elf helper.

00:04:15.190 --> 00:04:17.000
Please make this into a movie!

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:19.019
Professor Gorilla

00:04:19.019 --> 00:04:23.980
Batman has so many iconic enemies, from The
Joker, Catwoman and Penguin to Poison Ivy,

00:04:23.980 --> 00:04:28.480
Two-Face, and The Riddler, that the movies
just don't seem to have room for some of his

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:30.370
lesser known adversaries.

00:04:30.370 --> 00:04:35.760
Which is too bad, because that means we'll
probably never see Professor Gorilla in a

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:36.760
movie.

00:04:36.760 --> 00:04:41.400
Originally appearing in the Batman manga by
Jiro Kuwata that ran in Japan's Shonen Ace

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:48.880
magazine in the '60s, Professor Gorilla was…
well, he was exactly what he sounds like:

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:54.510
A gorilla who gained genius-level human intelligence
and then tried to exact revenge on the entire

00:04:54.510 --> 00:04:55.510
human race.

00:04:55.510 --> 00:05:00.170
Hey, if they're going to keep making Planet
of the Apes films, they might as well put

00:05:00.170 --> 00:05:01.830
Batman in one!

00:05:01.830 --> 00:05:03.350
Robin Dies At Dawn

00:05:03.350 --> 00:05:08.210
If you were looking for a great story that
dealt with the psychological trauma of bring

00:05:08.210 --> 00:05:14.280
Batman, you could do a lot worse than to go
back to the 1963 classic "Robin Dies At Dawn."

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:20.480
Arguably the best Batman story of the decade,
"Robin Dies at Dawn" shows a Batman who's

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:26.010
struggling with the paralyzing fear that his
life as a superhero will inevitably lead only

00:05:26.010 --> 00:05:31.160
to more death and suffering—specifically
to Robin's death, which would cause Batman

00:05:31.160 --> 00:05:34.130
to lose his only family a second time.

00:05:34.130 --> 00:05:39.620
Of course, it's also a story that opens with
Batman battling aliens in space and ends with

00:05:39.620 --> 00:05:44.860
him fighting crooks while wearing most of
a gorilla costume over his bat costume, so

00:05:44.860 --> 00:05:46.840
it has that going for it too.

00:05:46.840 --> 00:05:51.850
Unfortunately for modern film fans, some things
are so awesome, they can only exist in the

00:05:51.850 --> 00:05:53.710
Silver Age of Comics.

00:05:53.710 --> 00:05:56.630
Sorry, Rainbow Batman and Bat-Baby!

00:05:56.630 --> 00:05:57.630
Thanks for watching!

00:05:57.630 --> 00:06:00.330
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:00.330 --> 00:06:02.540
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

